and `patch`, and that is the act `git clean` is refused for (`G-10`) —
`git clean` deletes untracked work and so does this. It cannot be
settled from the path: `T-19` exists *because* `git rm` refuses
untracked files, so refusing them here would reimplement the problem,
and nothing distinguishes a scratch file the loop wrote this step from
one a person left in the tree. Asking is the only honest answer to a
question the harness cannot decide. This narrows `T-19` — the loop can
no longer tidy up unattended — and that is the trade.

**A citation that resolved to nothing.** The doc comment cited
`c4/b19/s01` as its evidence. That batch has no steps in the journal,
and the id has never appeared in any committed version of it. The
line above it cited `c2/b8/s06`; that batch holds three gate records at
`s08` and nothing else. Both removed.

Worth saying plainly: of 31 step citations in `crates/`, exactly one
resolves. Most are not inventions — the journal retains only the
closing steps of the early batches, so the cited work happened and its
records are gone. But a convention that cannot be checked is a
convention that will drift, and it did.

